s are structured how  questions are structured how pronouns  are used whether adjectives come before  or after the verb all this stuff you  will see some examples of it  which is not bad  however  i think we need to be realistic as to  how much this helps you  my biggest struggle when learning a  language is acquiring words thousands  and thousands of words so 12 sentences  where a lot of the same words repeat is  not helping helping me acquire lots of  vocabulary however it is useful and in  the many stories that we have at link a  lot of the basic structures of the  language do in fact appear  and and uh repeatedly so that we can  start getting used to them  just knowing what they are is is not the  same as getting used to the structures  of a new language and learning a  language is largely a matter of  acquiring new habits getting used to a  new way of saying things including  learning the words and phrases  phrases which very often embody the  grammar of the language and sometimes if  we're interested we'll look up the  underlying grammar rules which we at  least in my case i quickly forget  but by being exposed to enough of the  language over enough time  i get used to it and the most common  sort of structures and the basic word  order and how questions are asked and  how negatives are structured we  encounter that very early so in that  sense it may not really be necessary  to  take these 12 sentences and learn them  or study them or memorize them however  there's no harm in doing that and and  what he suggests is that this gives you  on one sheet of paper or one  screen  sort of a certain range of structures  but it's not the full range of  structures because in any language you  know  if we're talking you know in 12  sentences where you're trying to cover  pronouns and verbs and present and  future there's lots of different past  tenses in some languages  there can be some continuous there can  be subjunctive  there can be literary language there can  be so many different things that you  don't cover it  and the second thing is as part of this  whole getting used to the language  i always find that  when first confronted with a structure  in a new language i don't necessarily  believe that that's how they say it it's  like my friend who was studying chinese  with me when we discovered that the  chinese in order to say are you going  they say nichibuchu you go not go and  and said like really they say that but  like in russian when you know one year  is  two three or four years is  and five and more is lit and when you  first come across it's like are you  serious that's what they do  and yet after a while all of these  little aspects of the language start to  become with enough exposure  you know we start to get used to it so  what i wanted to say on this is that it  can be helpful  to take tim versus 12 sentences  translate them but of course if it's in  another writing system like even in in  the latin alphabet if i were to do this  say in finnish which i don't know at all  just to see 12 finnish sentences but i  don't know what any of the words mean is  doesn't necessarily do a lot for me  not to mention if we're talking about  say arabic  where i can't even read it if i'm new to  the language so  it's there it can help us but to me it's  not a significant secret to speeding up  the process of language learning and i  should point out by the way that tim  ferriss you know i've heard him speak a  number of languages and he speaks them  very well and everyone has their own  favorite  thing that they do  that they believe helps them learn  languages better and they enjoy doing  them and therefore they're effective for  them  but i just feel that as a general  statement  learning a language is such a gradual  process of of getting acclimatized to a  new linguistic environment that just  knowing 12 sentences certain basic  structures word order negatives question  marks  it doesn't take you  a long way into the language because  actually to be good at a language you  need a lot of words and as i constantly  say that requires a lot of listening and  reading and eventually using the  language  and maybe when you go to use it to  refresh your memory you can go to this  these 12 um sample sentences that it  might help you but again when speaking i  don't think you'd want to be referring  back to a particular you know list of  sample sentences you want to be relying  on the fact that you've had so much  listening and reading that certain  patterns and structures in the language  just come out naturally in the form of  words and phrases that you have  basically absorbed so there you have it  my take on  tim ferriss deconstructing a language in  order to learn it thank you for  listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

